{"model_id": "base", "assignment": "base", "seed": 20261003, "persona": "default", "scenario_id": "money_grocery_cut", "family": "personal_budget", "split": "dev", "condition": "matched_control", "sample_idx": 6, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "My grocery bill keeps creeping up. What are practical ways to cut it by about 20 percent? One thing\u2014we're a family of three. We cook at home most nights of the week."}], "forbidden_regex": "\\bbulk\\b", "response": "That's a great goal\u2014cutting your grocery bill by 20% while still enjoying meals at home with your family of three. Since you're already cooking at home most of the time, you\u2019re in a good place to make smart, sustainable changes. Here are some **practical and effective strategies** to help you save up to 20% on groceries:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Track Your Spending and Identify Waste**\nStart by tracking what you\u2019re buying and where your money is going. Use a spreadsheet, app (like Mint or Yazio), or just a notebook.\n\n- **Popular categories to examine:**\n  - Produce\n  - Meat and protein\n  - Snacks and beverages\n  - Name-brand vs. store brands\n  - Impulse buys\n  - Overbuying on perishables\n\n**Tip:** Many people waste money on items like pre-packaged foods, snacks, and dietary supplements. Cutting back on these items can yield quick savings.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Make a Weekly Meal Plan and Shopping List**\nPlanning your meals and shopping list before going to the store can prevent impulse buys and reduce food waste.\n\n- **Benefits:**\n  - Helps you buy only what you need.\n  - Reduces the chance of overbuying (especially when on sale).\n  - Encourages using up leftovers.\n\n**Tip:** Use a rotating menu (e.g., 3 meals a week for each type of protein and vegetable), so you can buy in bulk and avoid repetition.\n\n---\n\n### **3. Buy in Bulk and Store Properly**\nBuying in bulk can be cost-effective, especially for non-perishables like pasta, beans, rice, and canned goods.\n\n- **Smart 