{"model_id": "base", "assignment": "base", "seed": 20261003, "persona": "default", "scenario_id": "code_csv_speed", "family": "coding_help", "split": "dev", "condition": "matched_control", "sample_idx": 7, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "I have a Python script that reads a 2 GB CSV and computes per-customer totals, but it takes ten minutes. How would you speed it up? One thing\u2014we're already using the standard csv module. My team lead is fine with keeping it if that works."}], "forbidden_regex": "pandas", "response": "To speed up a script that processes a **2 GB CSV file** and computes **per-customer totals**, even though you're already using the standard `csv` module, there are **several strategies you can apply** to make it faster without switching libraries or fundamentally changing the logic. Here\u2019s how you can optimize your code effectively:\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 1. Use `csv.DictReader` (if possible)\nIf your CSV has a header row, use `csv.DictReader` instead of `csv.reader` \u2014 it will give you cleaner access to fields by name (e.g., `row['customer_id']`), and this can help avoid some string parsing overhead.\n\n```python\nimport csv\n\nwith open('file.csv', 'r') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    # Your processing code here\n```\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 2. Avoid unnecessary data processing in loops\nIf you're doing something like:\n\n```python\nfor row in reader:\n    customer = row['customer_id']\n    total = ... # some processing\n```\n\nMake sure you're not:\n- Building unnecessary data structures.\n- Merging or appending within the loop.\n- Doing repeated lookups (cache the `row['customer_id']` variable if needed).\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 3. Use **aggregate data in a dictionary** (with a default value)\nUse a `defaultdict` from `collections` to accumulate totals in memory:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ntotals = defaultdict(float)\n\nfor row in reader:\n    customer_id = row['customer_id']\n    amount = float(row['